<article class="env-lemma" id="0544">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0544">Lemma <span data-tag="0544">10.32.3</span>.</a> Let $R \to R'$ be a ring map and let $I \subset R$ be a locally nilpotent ideal. Then $IR'$ is a locally nilpotent ideal of $R'$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      This follows from the fact that if $x, y \in R'$ are nilpotent, then $x + y$ is nilpotent too. Namely, if $x^ n = 0$ and $y^ m = 0$, then $(x + y)^{n + m - 1} = 0$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>